{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:scout","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:eb36331aa7b44db82bb4ad06a8ff996bdcbf2c3c6fcf86d3899c40e2d87dd9fe","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.8},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.8},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.7}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"REFERENCE CLASS: 5-year PPP-weighted realized growth averages for the OECD+China bloc, anchored on the current observed run-rate and walked forward by the locked demographic deltas.\n\nPPP WEIGHTS CARRIED EXPLICITLY. 2025 world PPP shares: OECD-38 ~46-47%, China ~19.5%. Within the bloc: OECD 0.705 / China 0.295 today; by 2032-36 China's share drifts up to ~0.32 because it still grows faster. I use 0.68 / 0.32.\n\nCURRENT OBSERVED RUN-RATE: IMF Oct-2026 WEO puts global growth at 3.1% for 2026 (raised 0.1pp) rebounding to 3.4% in 2027, with US growth \"expected to remain at 2.3 percent\" and EU/euro area \"subdued\". Caveat: sources disagree on whether the 2027 figure was revised +0.2pp or left unchanged, and I found NO October-2026-edition figure for China or for the AE aggregate — the 4.5% China number in circulation comes from the January-2026 update, not October. So the bloc run-rate is INFERRED: 0.68 x ~1.7 + 0.32 x ~4.6 = ~2.6%/yr.\n\nWALK-FORWARD, OECD-38 LEG. Locked 2A: aging cuts baseline OECD real GDP per capita growth ~40%, 1.0%->0.6%/yr by 2036. Add OECD-38 population growth ~+0.2%/yr (US +0.3-0.4% on lower immigration, EU ~0, Japan/Korea negative) = 0.8%/yr absent productivity offsets. AI offset: expected PPP-weighted TFP lift from the locked 2B regime masses = 0.162 x ~0.75 + 0.472 x ~0.35 + 0.365 x ~0.07 = +0.31pp/yr. JONES/BAUMOL WEAKEST-LINK HAIRCUT: pass-through to measured output is at most 1:1 and plausibly less, because the binding tasks are observably the non-automated physical ones. Where the cap binds is directly measurable TODAY: transformer lead times >160 weeks (vs 143 weeks average in 2024), gas turbine lead times five years after 43 GW of 2025 orders (strongest in two decades), ERCOT large-load interconnection queue ~410 GW in April 2026 (about five times the grid's total generating capacity — caveat preserved: \"developers seek to hedge their bets due to lengthy and uncertain electricity interconnection queues\", so queues are padded with duplicates and this number overstates real load), lead time to power 36 months in Pittsburgh to 84 months in Columbus, Ohio. A task that cannot be energized before 2032 cannot contribute TFP by 2036. Also subtract: the capex-level growth contribution goes to ~zero or negative in 2032-36 given the locked 66% AI-Q4 impairment/revenue-shortfall prior, plus fiscal drag (net interest 2%->3% of GDP) and crowding out at a 2.60% US 10y real yield. OECD-38 P50 = 1.25%/yr (1.7 today, -0.45pp).\n\nWALK-FORWARD, CHINA LEG — this is where the PPP weight does the work. Locked 2A: -2.7pp/yr aging drag, population -60M over 2026-2035 (7.92M births in 2025 vs deaths rising toward ~15M/yr, so roughly -0.5%/yr headcount and -1.0 to -1.3%/yr working-age), worker-to-retiree 4.4->2.8, pension/social-security subsidies ~2.9T RMB (~10.1% of fiscal expenditure). China is also the world's largest automation adopter, so the labour-scarcity DEMAND PULL partially offsets. From ~4.6% today to P50 2.8%/yr in 2032-36, i.e. -1.8pp realized against a -2.7pp gross aging drag, with ~0.9pp recovered through capital deepening and automation. 0.32 x 2.8 = 0.90pp of the bloc answer — China alone contributes half the total.\n\nCOMBINED P50 = 0.68 x 1.25 + 0.32 x 2.8 = 0.85 + 0.90 = 1.75%, rounded to 1.8%/yr. This is the key coupled finding: the locked 2A demographic block MECHANICALLY delivers a bloc P50 below 2%, roughly 0.8pp below the current observed run-rate, before any AI-pessimism is added.\n\nP10 = 0.8: requires a capex bust (66% prior) landing inside the window plus a China hard landing plus two sub-zero OECD years; 5-year averages smooth, so one -1% year with 2% elsewhere still gives ~1.4%, and 0.8 needs two.\nP90 = 2.7: requires TFP breakout AND China holding ~4.0% AND OECD ~2.0% (0.68 x 2.0 + 0.32 x 4.0 = 2.64). Distribution left-skewed (P50-P10 = 1.0 vs P90-P50 = 0.9) to respect the locked impairment prior.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.166Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Atlanta Fed Wage Growth Tracker, 12-month moving average of median hourly wage growth: the Construction-and-Mining series MINUS the Finance-and-Business-Services series. Current observed state is a dead heat on the closest available proxies (Education and Health 4.2 in Sep-2025; Finance and Business Services 4.2 in Feb-2026; Construction and Mining latest value unverified at my horizon). Watch for a gap exceeding +1.5pp sustained across four consecutive quarters. Caveat: these are broad industry groups, not ISIC F/Q/J/K/M, and they are unweighted medians.\",\"by_when\":\"Q4 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 rises from 12 to roughly 25-30 (the second conjunct's marginal goes from 16% to ~40%); hypothesis plank (a) rises from 7 to ~25; CQ6-B gains ~4 points from C and D. If instead the gap is still under +0.5pp by Q4 2029, CQ1 falls to ~7 and plank (a) to ~4.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Realized 2027 hyperscaler capex versus the Oct-2026 guidance, together with disclosed AI-asset impairments or cancelled buildouts in 10-K/20-F filings. Observed baseline: 2026 forecast $803-828B across Amazon, Alphabet, Meta, Microsoft and SpaceX, with Amazon's 2026 cash capex ~$220B and 'meaningfully more in 2027', Microsoft's CY outlook ~$170B and revised DOWN because third-party data-centre capacity was delayed, Alphabet 2026 at $180-190B and 2027 'significantly' higher, Meta still building its 2027 budget as of the 21 Oct 2026 call. Caveat: the $803-828B and earlier ~$725B figures use different company sets and dates and are not comparable; I did not verify against filings.\",\"by_when\":\"December 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Realized 2027 capex >= $950B with zero material impairment disclosures: CQ3 P50 +0.2pp to 2.0, CQ6-A to ~18 and CQ6-D to ~14, CQ4 P50 +0.3pp to 2.4 on crowding out. Cumulative cancellations/impairments >= 20% of announced buildouts: CQ3 P50 -0.3pp to 1.5, CQ6-D to ~28, CQ6-A to ~7, and long nominal bonds move to the top of the 2031-36 asset ranking.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI, NBS monthly release. Observed: +0.9% y/y in September 2026 (released 14 Oct 2026), up from +0.8% in August and +0.5% in July; core ex-food-and-energy 1.0%, reported as the first 1.0% in 19 months; PPI +5.0% y/y accelerating from +3.8%. The PPI acceleration is the leading indicator. Watch for headline CPI above 2.0% y/y for six consecutive months.\",\"by_when\":\"December 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ2 falls from 26 to ~12 (conjunct B from 58% to ~25%); CQ6-C rises ~3 points and CQ6-D falls ~4; China equities move up and China onshore duration down inside the asset matrix. Conversely, headline CPI back below 0.3% y/y for six consecutive months pushes CQ2 to ~34 and CQ6-D to ~24.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE Index, 12-month trailing average, taken from ICE's own index data rather than an aggregator. Current level unverified at my horizon: the most recent value I trust is 70.9 on 17 Jul 2026 (ICE BofA via Yahoo Finance, a third-party aggregator), against a dated 103.01 on 26 Mar 2026 and 'above 170' during the April 2025 basis-trade stress; these cannot all be current. Watch for the trailing 12-month average printing >= 110 in two consecutive calendar years.\",\"by_when\":\"December 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ5 rises from 16 to roughly 30 (conjunct A from 32% to ~65%); CQ4 P90 rises from 3.1 to ~3.5; CQ6-C rises ~3 points; G7 long nominal bonds get downgraded across every regime column. If instead the trailing average stays below 85 through 2030, CQ5 falls to ~8.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Census Bureau Business Trends and Outlook Survey national AI use rate, firms with at least 20 employees, read off the Census BTOS data tool directly rather than secondary trackers. Observed: 19.8% national as of 3 May 2026, with Information 39.7% and Finance and Insurance 33.9%; 'Between December 2025 and May 2026, AI use increased among firms with at least 20 employees but didn't change significantly among firms with fewer than 20 employees'. Caveats preserved: in November 2025 Census broadened the questions to include use of AI 'in any of its business functions', so the later jump should not be read as pure adoption growth, and the 21.7% (July 2026) and 23.8% (24 Aug-6 Sep 2026) figures in circulation are secondary and I could not confirm them against Census releases. Watch the post-break series crossing 40%.\",\"by_when\":\"December 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The locked AI-Q2 marginal (21.3% on >=50% of OECD-38 firms by 2036) would be clearing from below, so CQ6-A rises from 12 to ~20 and CQ6-D falls from 19 to ~13; CQ3 P50 rises ~0.2pp; the AI/hyperscaler complex's 2031-36 B-regime number rises ~2pp. If the post-break series is still under 30% in Dec 2030, CQ6-A falls to ~7.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 scout seat, effort 'growth'."}}